  "content": {
    "id": "p9-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Show that the sum of four consecutive integers is divisible by 2, but not by 4.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The sum is $4n+6=2(2n+3)=4(n+1)+2$.\n\nIt is a multiple of 2 and leaves remainder 2 when divided by 4.",
      "worked": "Let the integers be $n$, $n+1$, $n+2$ and $n+3$, where $n$ is an integer.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}n+(n+1)+(n+2)+(n+3)&=4n+6\\\\n+(n+1)+(n+2)+(n+3)&=2(2n+3)\\\\n+(n+1)+(n+2)+(n+3)&=4(n+1)+2.\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $2n+3$ is an integer, the sum is divisible by 2. It leaves remainder 2 when divided by 4, so it is not divisible by 4.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 52
  },
